{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>Whoever hunts game, etc.</b> One needs to know for what matter the verse found it necessary to write all this, for it should have said only ״a man who slaughters an animal or״ etc., or ״who pours blood״ etc. Further, if the inference of the words is the commandment of covering, it should have said ״he shall cover it with dust,״ for saying ״and he shall cover it״ implies that another commandment preceded it." "It appears that in saying <b>whoever hunts</b> the verse intended to prohibit hunting an unclean species ab initio, even though the verse permitted deriving benefit from them, as I raised in my work Pri To'ar, siman 117, following the view of Rambam (Forbidden Foods, chapter 8) that there is no permission to hunt them unless one intends to hunt clean animals and unclean ones happen along with them — and this is the statement of the verse here: <b>whoever hunts</b> etc. — it is a condition, the matter being that it permits him to hunt only <b>that may be eaten</b> and not that may not be eaten. And it says <b>and he shall cover it</b> because, since another commandment preceded it — not to hunt that which may not be eaten — it is fitting to say ״and he shall cover it״ with the addition of the vav. And it says <b>and poured</b> etc., meaning, that it is forbidden to eat it, in the manner that it stated in another place (Deuteronomy 12:16): ״you shall not eat it; on the earth you shall pour it like water.״ Therefore it concluded by saying here <b>for the life of all flesh</b> and finishing <b>and I have said</b> etc. — thus you have learned that in the statement <b>and poured</b> the intention was to prohibit eating the blood of animal and fowl. You then find that three commandments were stated here: (1) he shall not hunt unclean animals to derive benefit from them; (2) he shall not eat the blood of animal and fowl; (3) a positive commandment to cover the blood of animal and fowl." "<b>Further,</b> it appears that He alluded, in His saying <b>and poured and covered it</b>, that one need not cover all its blood, but rather part through pouring and part through covering (Ḥullin 84a)."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {1 "Pri To'ar appears to be the Or HaChaim's own halakhic work; the citation format follows the original."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:57:54.873171656Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}